@namespace Fhi.Munin.Explorer.Blazor

@* No @page — the host owns routing; in helsedata's Optimizely CMS there is no router at all.
   No @rendermode — the host decides at the mount site, which is what lets this same
   component render under legacy Blazor Server and under a modern Blazor Web App.
   No CSS ships from this package.

   Every class name below that is meant to be styled is one helsedata already defines, off one of
   their two compiled stylesheets: Fhi.Helsedata.Stiler site-wide for the ordinary page furniture,
   and `variables.css` — which despite the name every page of helsedata.no loads — for the whole
   result vocabulary. Since `Fhi.Metadata-zs56s` the results are their own variable page's rows
   (`munin-explorer-data-list*`, `munin-explorer-dataitem-*`) in the list that page builds around them
   (`munin-explorer-container`, `munin-explorer-results`, `munin-explorer-header*`), with
   `munin-explorer-meta*` for the panel an open row shows and `munin-explorer-pagination*` for the
   pager. Nothing is left wearing a name from that file. The pager and the skip link into it held
   out longest, because Stiler had no pagination rule at all and their stylesheet did:
   `Fhi.Metadata-hyyxl` moved the pager into the prefix with everything else and
   `Fhi.Metadata-ja2qu` moved the link, as `munin-explorer-skiplink-pagination`. The
   searchbox shape — submit button inside the field — is still lifted from helsedata's own
   datakildeutforsker (Features/DataSources/DataSourceComponent); its `datasourcecard*` card list
   is not, and no `datasourcecard` name is emitted here any more.

   Inventing `variabelutforsker-*` names — which is what this used to do, twice — meant the host
   stylesheet had never heard of them, so the Søk button, the search field and the table headers
   rendered as raw browser defaults inside an otherwise styled page. Matching helsedata without
   guessing is the entire reason this ships as a component, so the names that carry styling have
   to be theirs. (The historical spelling is deliberate: it records the mistake as it was made.)

   The rest of the `munin-explorer-*` prefix is ours and is not that mistake, because neither
   stylesheet is expected to have a rule for it. Most of those names are handles — the root
   `munin-explorer` class below, the DOM ids the codebehind mints, and `-filters`, `-detail`,
   `-drilldown`, `-kodeverk*`, `-codes*`, `-group` and the nine `-kilde*` names in KildeView — on
   elements something else already dresses, a Stiler class they also wear or their own browser
   default, so an undefined one costs look and not information. Two are not handles and a host
   outside helsedata has to draw them itself: `munin-explorer-crumb`, which is the only sign
   the kilde step of the trail can be pressed, and `munin-explorer-period__track` / `__fill` /
   `__track--ongoing`, which are the period bar itself. README lists every one of them; see also
   the comment above the `<section>`.

   The result vocabulary is a dependency a host outside helsedata's estate has to meet in full,
   and the component class's remarks say so — including the rule that keeps
   `munin-explorer-skiplink-pagination` out of sight until it is focused, which ships in Stiler
   0.1.14.

   Accessibility: helsedata.no is a public-sector site, so WCAG 2.1 AA is a legal
   requirement — and this is our markup on their page. The choices below are the ones
   markup can make. Visible focus and contrast are the host stylesheet's to get right, and
   are spelled out for hosts in the doc comment on the component class in the codebehind. *@

@* One of the few names here that is ours rather than helsedata's — the others are
   `munin-explorer-filters`, `-detail`, `-drilldown` and `-group` further down. None of them
   carry styling: nothing in this package, in Stiler or in helsedata's `variables.css` defines a
   rule for any of them. They exist so the component, and the parts of it a host or a test wants
   to reach, can be found in the DOM of a CMS page that renders a hundred other things. *@
<section class="munin-explorer" aria-labelledby="@TitleId">
    @* Rendered from HeadingLevel rather than hard-coded to h2: the level that keeps the
       document outline unbroken is only knowable at the mount site. *@
    @Heading

@* A kilde or datasamling opens as its own view, not as a third panel inside an open row.
   Runa navigates to a page for this; this component has no router, so instead of nesting a
   panel inside a panel inside a row it takes over its own area and offers a way back. The
   reader gets a full width to read in, which three levels of nesting cannot give, and the
   search, filters, page and open row are all still there on return because none of it was
   torn down. *@
@if (_wholeVariable && _detail is { } whole)
{
    @* The whole variable, in place. Same shape as the kilde drill-in beneath it, and for the same
       reason: with no router, a detail page is a view this component swaps to rather than a route
       the host has to own. *@
    <div class="munin-explorer-drilldown"
         id="@WholeVariableId"
         role="region"
         aria-labelledby="@WholeVariableHeadingId">
        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-bottom"
                type="button"
                @onclick="ToggleWholeVariableAsync">@T.BackToVariables</button>

        <VariableView Variable="whole"
                      Language="@Language"
                      HeadingLevel="@RowLevel"
                      HeadingId="@WholeVariableHeadingId"
                      Sections="@KodeverkSection(whole)" />
    </div>
}
else if (_sourceKind is not null)
{
    @* A region named after what it shows, so a screen reader moving by landmark finds it and hears
       which kilde it landed in. The old nested panel carried these; taking them off when it became
       a view would have quietly removed it from the landmark list. *@
    <div class="munin-explorer-drilldown"
         id="@SourceId"
         role="region"
         aria-labelledby="@SourceHeadingId"
         aria-busy="@SourceBusy">
        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-bottom"
                type="button"
                @onclick="CloseSourceAsync">@T.BackToVariables</button>

        @* Once a kilde has arrived, KildeView owns the heading - the same component Kelda renders
           on a page of its own, so the two cannot drift. Until then, and for a datasamling, the
           drill-in keeps its own heading: the region points at it by id, and a landmark whose label
           does not exist yet is worse than a plain one. *@
        @if (_kilde is null)
        {
            @DrilldownHeading
        }

        <p class="@SourceStatusClass"
           role="status"
           aria-live="polite"
           aria-atomic="true">@SourceStatus</p>

        @if (_kilde is { } kilde)
        {
            <KildeView Kilde="kilde"
                       Language="@Language"
                       HeadingLevel="@RowLevel"
                       HeadingId="@SourceHeadingId" />
        }
        else
        {
            @SourceFields
        }
    </div>
}
else
{

    @* role="search" makes this a search landmark, and aria-labelledby names it after this
       instance's title so two explorers on one page are told apart in a landmark list. *@
    <form role="search"
          aria-labelledby="@TitleId"
          @onsubmit="SearchAsync"
          @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
        @* A visible label, deliberately, where helsedata's datakildeutforsker uses a bare
           aria-label. `form-element__label` is Stiler's own label style, so it costs nothing
           to keep the text on screen — and a visible label serves far more people than an
           invisible one. *@
        <label class="form-element__label" for="@SearchId">@T.SearchLabel</label>

        @* searchbox__freetext reserves 104px of right padding for a button sitting inside the
           field, which is what searchbox__freetext-submit-button positions there. Both come
           from Stiler; this is the arrangement their own search box uses. *@
        @* The field and its clear button on one line. The wrapper is what lets a host shorten
           the box and put the button beside it rather than under it — see the button below. *@
        <div class="munin-explorer-search">
        <div class="searchbox__freetext-container">
            @* @bind (onchange), deliberately NOT value + @oninput.

               This renders inside helsedata.no's Blazor Server circuit, so @oninput means one
               round-trip per keystroke — for a search that only ever runs on submit, that is
               traffic for nothing, multiplied by every visitor on a public site. Worse, each
               round-trip re-renders and rewrites the input while more input is still arriving:
               a fast paste or a programmatic fill loses characters (typing "svelging" quickly
               arrived as "sng").

               onchange is enough because searching is always an explicit act here: it fires on
               implicit submit (Enter), and a click on Søk blurs the field first, so the change
               event reaches the circuit before @onsubmit does. The event is spelled out rather
               than left implicit so nobody "helpfully" restores oninput.

               type="text", not type="search": the user-agent ✕ fires the DOM `search` event, which
               Blazor does not know, so it emptied the box without applying it. enterkeyhint keeps
               the soft keyboard's search key. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
            <input class="searchbox__freetext"
                   id="@SearchId"
                   type="text"
                   enterkeyhint="search"
                   @bind="_search"
                   @bind:event="onchange"
                   placeholder="@T.SearchPlaceholder"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   maxlength="200" />
            @* Deliberately NOT disabled while a search runs. Disabling the element that
               currently has focus drops focus to <body>, so a keyboard user who just pressed
               Enter on Søk loses their place and starts tabbing from the top of the page
               again. SearchAsync ignores a second submit while one is in flight instead, which
               stops the double request without touching focus. *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--primary searchbox__freetext-submit-button"
                    type="submit">@T.SearchButton</button>
        </div>

            @* aria-disabled, not disabled: a disabled button cannot hold focus, so pressing this
               one would clear the search and throw focus to the document. The attribute stops no
               click, so the handler refuses instead. (Fhi.Metadata-5ghur) *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--secondary munin-explorer-search__clear"
                    type="button"
                    aria-disabled="@(!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_search) ? "false" : "true")"
                    @onclick="ClearSearchAsync">@T.ClearSearch</button>
        </div>
    </form>

    @* The filters.

       A stack of disclosures above the results, not a sidebar beside them. A sidebar is a layout,
       and a layout is a class name — helsedata's own variable page styles one from
       `filter-search-explorer` in its page-specific variables.css, a rule this repository has not
       read back — the result vocabulary below comes from that same stylesheet, so what is
       unverified here is the one name and not the file. The standing rule is that a class name
       goes into this markup only once it has been read off the host's compiled CSS, so the panel
       is built out of names that have been: `form-fieldset` and `form-element__label` from the
       sort control, `hd-button-square` with the same filled/ghost pair, `caption` and `infobox`.
       When those sidebar names can be verified, this block is what moves into it — the markup
       below is already a list of facets and nothing about it assumes it is stacked.

       Two shapes here are elements rather than classes, for the same reason:

       * `<details>` per facet. With every facet expanded the panel is hundreds of buttons; Munin's
         explorer uses an accordion, and `<details>` is the one this package can have — native
         disclosure behaviour, no script, no class name, and the browser draws the marker. Its open
         state lives in the browser, so filtering does not collapse what the reader opened: the
         `open` attribute below is a constant per facet, and a constant is never patched.
       * A bare `<ul>` for the values, nested for the hierarchy. Indentation is what shows a delkilde
         sitting under its kilde, and a list is an element every base stylesheet already styles —
         unlike `variabelutforsker-tree`, which would be exactly the invented name that renders as a
         raw browser default. Values are toggle buttons rather than checkboxes for the same reason:
         Stiler has a square button and this component already draws one in two states.

       `munin-explorer-filters` carries no styling, in this package or in Stiler. It is the same
       kind of name as the `munin-explorer` root above it: a hook for a host that wants to place
       the panel, and nothing this markup relies on. *@
    @if (_facets is not null)
    {
        <fieldset class="form-fieldset munin-explorer-filters" aria-busy="@Busy">
            <legend class="form-element__label">@FiltersLegend</legend>

            @foreach (var group in FacetGroups)
            {
                @* Keyed, so a facet that drops out of the list — the API omits one with no matches
                   — cannot hand its open state to whichever facet takes its place. *@
                <details @key="group.Key" open="@group.OpenByDefault">
                    <summary><span class="form-element__label">@GroupLabel(group)</span></summary>
                    @if (group.Values.Count > 0)
                    {
                        @FacetList(group.Values)
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <p class="caption">@group.EmptyText</p>
                    }
                </details>
            }

            @* Never taken off the page, only made inert — the same treatment the pager's buttons
               get, and for the same reason: clearing the filters from a button that then disappears
               drops focus to <body>. aria-disabled says so; ClearFiltersAsync is what makes it true. *@
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-right margin-bottom"
                    type="button"
                    aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(!_filter.IsEmpty)"
                    @onclick="ClearFiltersAsync">@T.ClearFilters</button>
        </fieldset>
    }

    @* The hierarchy trail, above the results and below the filters — which is where Runa puts it,
       and the only place it can say "these rows are what is left of that kilde".

       Drawn only while something in the hierarchy is chosen. There is no trail to a selection
       nobody has made, and a permanent empty one would be furniture in the same way "Side 1 av 1"
       between two dead pager buttons is. It is built from the filter rather than from a second
       piece of state, so it cannot describe a selection the rows below it did not come from — see
       the remarks in the codebehind for why every step is a button and why the × is not one of
       them. *@
    @if (HierarchyCrumbs is { Count: > 0 } crumbs)
    {
        @Breadcrumb(crumbs)
    }

    @* The ordering used to live in a "Sorter etter" fieldset here. It moved into the column
       header, which is where helsedata and Runa both put it, and where a reader looks for it:
       the header cell is the button, and it carries the aria-sort. Keeping both would offer the
       same choice twice. *@

    @* One polite status line, holding one message at a time: loading, the result summary,
       or "no matches". aria-atomic makes a screen reader read the whole new sentence rather
       than only the words that changed — "12" on its own is not information.

       Loading is announced too. That is one extra utterance per search, which is the price
       of telling a screen-reader user that pressing Søk did something at all when the API
       is slow; the region is shared rather than stacked so the messages replace each other
       instead of piling up.

       `caption` is Stiler's muted secondary-text style. It also carries the empty state: a
       full-page emptystate block would be out of proportion inside a component that is one
       section of somebody else's page, and duplicating the sentence outside the live region
       would put it on screen twice. *@
    <p class="caption" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_loading)
        {
            @T.Loading
        }
        else if (_result is { Items.Count: > 0 })
        {
            @Summary
        }
        else if (_result is not null)
        {
            @T.NoResults(_executedSearch, _filter.ActiveCount)
        }
    </p>

    @* The failure is worth interrupting for, so it is an alert rather than a polite update.
       The container is always rendered, empty when there is nothing wrong: a role="alert"
       element that is inserted and filled in the same DOM update is announced unreliably,
       while one that is already there when its text arrives is not. It carries no class of
       its own precisely because it is always present — an `infobox` here would draw an empty
       coloured box on every page that has nothing to report. *@
    <div role="alert" aria-live="assertive" aria-atomic="true">
        @if (_error is not null)
        {
            <p class="infobox infobox--bg-yellow">@_error</p>
        }
        @* Inside the region and not beside it, so the failure and the way out of it are announced
           in one utterance rather than the interruption saying only that something went wrong.

           It outlives the message it answers by exactly one fetch: a successful retry clears the
           sentence, and a button leaving with it would drop the focus of the reader who just
           pressed it to <body> — the pager's rule, and Fjern alle filtre's. The next fetch someone
           else starts takes it away whether it answers or is refused for asking too often, so a
           dead offer is not read out again with every later failure in this aria-atomic region.

           munin-explorer-retry is what draws the inert state. The region carries no class, so
           neither the pager's nor the filter panel's [aria-disabled] rule reaches in here, and
           without one of its own a button that does nothing looks exactly like one that does.

           That rule has to ship in Fhi.Helsedata.Stiler under components/munin-explorer/, which as
           of 0.1.14 carries none: the sample stylesheets are the samples' own, so a Stiler-only
           host draws the two states alike until it does. Tracked as Fhi.Metadata-x6vqc. *@
        @if (_failedRows is not null)
        {
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-right margin-bottom munin-explorer-retry"
                    type="button"
                    aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(_retryRowsEnabled)"
                    @onclick="RetryRowsAsync">@T.RetrySearch</button>
        }
        @* The facets failing is a different failure from the search failing — the rows are the right
           rows and it is the numbers beside the filters that may be stale — but it shares this one
           alert region rather than opening a second. Two assertive regions on one component race to
           be announced, and the reader has no way to tell which one spoke. *@
        @if (_facetError is not null)
        {
            <p class="infobox infobox--bg-yellow">@_facetError</p>
        }
        @* Its own button under its own sentence, named for what it retries: both failures can be
           reported at once, and two buttons reading "Prøv igjen" in one assertive region would be
           two identical offers with nothing to choose between them. *@
        @if (_retryFacetsShown)
        {
            <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-right margin-bottom munin-explorer-retry"
                    type="button"
                    aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(_retryFacetsEnabled)"
                    @onclick="RetryFacetsAsync">@T.RetryFilters</button>
        }
    </div>

    @* The container and its header render whether or not anything was found. The ordering lives
       in that header, and hiding it when a search comes back empty would take the button the reader
       just pressed out of the document — the same reason the Søk button is never disabled. *@
    <div class="munin-explorer-container">
    <div class="munin-explorer-results">
    @* The column picker, above the list and inside the results column — which is where
       helsedata's own variable page puts it, in what they call `variable-explorer-header__actions`. Not inside
       `munin-explorer-data-list__header` where the bead expected it: that row is the columns themselves,
       and it is the one element the picker must be able to empty without emptying itself. Not
       `sortable-dropdown` either, though the bead named it — see the remarks on ColumnPicker: that
       is their mobile sort control and it is `display: none` above 1280px.

       Rendered whether or not anything was found, for the reason the header is: a control that
       leaves the document when a search comes back empty takes the reader's place in it with it. *@
    @ColumnPicker()

    @if (_result is { Items.Count: > 0 } && ShowPager)
    {
        @* Without this a keyboard user reaches Neste by tabbing past every card on the page —
           one stop per row, which the reader can now set as high as fifty. The rule the host
           supplies for `munin-explorer-skiplink-pagination` keeps it out of sight until it is
           focused, which is the one place this markup depends on the host hiding something: see
           the note on the component class. The name was helsedata's `skiplink-pagination` until
           `Fhi.Metadata-ja2qu`, and no released Stiler ever had a rule that hid it, so on a
           Stiler-only host the link sat permanently visible over every multi-page result list.

           Rendered only alongside a pager, because with a single page there is nothing to skip to
           and the link would be a tab stop leading nowhere.

           Above the table now, not between its header and its rows. A `role="table"` may own
           nothing but rows and rowgroups, so a link inside it is a structure error — axe reports
           it, and a reader walking the table meets a link where a row should be. It still sits
           beside the list it skips and is still invisible until focused. *@
        <a class="munin-explorer-skiplink-pagination" href="#@PaginationId">@T.SkipToPagination</a>
    }

    @* role="table" over the header row and the result rows together.

       Until this was written the list had no table semantics at all: measured in Chrome's
       accessibility tree against the sample stylesheet, every row, column and header of it
       resolved to `generic`, there was no `sort` property anywhere in the document, and the
       `aria-sort` the sorted header carries sat on an element with no role that may carry one.
       That is WCAG 1.3.1 and 4.1.2, both Level A.

       Roles rather than a real <table>, which is what Kelda uses: `munin-explorer-dataitem-main`
       is `display: flex` in Stiler, and flex on a <table> is exactly what strips table semantics
       from it. Explicit ARIA roles survive it — verified in the accessibility tree with the
       stylesheet applied, not in the markup — which is why the layout wrappers below wear
       `role="none"` instead: a row may own nothing but cells, so an element that is only there to
       lay the columns out has to step aside rather than sit in the tree between them.

       aria-label rather than aria-labelledby: the name is the same sentence the status line above
       shows, and pointing at that element would make the table's name change under a screen
       reader every time the status line does. *@
    <div class="munin-explorer-data-list__result" role="table" aria-label="@Summary">
    @ResultHeader()

    @if (_result is { Items.Count: > 0 })
    {
        @* helsedata's own variable page builds its result grid from a <ul> and a header row of
           <div>s, not a <table> — there is not a single <table> element on the page. The column
           layout is theirs; what this markup has to get right is the nesting, because 132 of the
           292 selectors covering these names are descendant selectors. A class placed at the wrong
           depth still renders, it just renders unstyled, which looks identical to a class that
           matched something empty.

           role="rowgroup", which is what a <tbody> is: the <ul> keeps laying the rows out and
           stops claiming to be a list, since a list inside a table is a structure the reader has
           to listen past. The name moved up to the table with it — one element names the whole
           thing, and a rowgroup carrying the same sentence would have it read twice. *@
        <ul class="munin-explorer-data-list" role="rowgroup" aria-busy="@Busy">
            @foreach (var v in _result.Items)
            {
                @* Keyed by the variable's own id, the same reason the facet values are keyed: a row
                   carries a control the reader can be standing on — the disclosure below — and the
                   rows reorder whenever the search, the filter or the ordering moves. Without a key
                   the renderer would patch that button into a different variable's rather than
                   moving it. *@
                @* role="row" on the list item, not on the flex container inside it: the open
                   panel is the item's second child, and a row owns everything under it — so the
                   row has to start above both, or the panel ends up beside the rows rather than
                   in one. The two wrappers below then wear role="none" and the panel becomes the
                   row's last cell. *@
                <li class="@RowItemClass(v)" role="row" @key="v.Id">
                    @* The row is the disclosure. helsedata makes the variable's own name the
                       button — button.munin-explorer-dataitem-main__name carrying aria-expanded — which
                       is the APG accordion pattern, and it is what removes the dead affordance the
                       old card had: a pointer cursor inherited from .datasourcecard, which is a
                       link on their datakilde page and was never a link here.

                       There is no heading around it. One was tried, so results could be walked
                       with a screen reader's heading rotor — their selectors all match by descent,
                       so an element in between styles fine. But their row is a flex container and
                       the name cell is sized by `munin-explorer-dataitem-main__name`, so a heading
                       becomes the flex item and the column stops lining up with its header.
                       Neither reference wraps it. *@
                    @* Both wrappers are layout and nothing else — one is helsedata's row box, the
                       other the flex container that lays the columns out — so both step out of the
                       accessibility tree and let the cells inside become the row's own. *@
                    <div class="munin-explorer-data-list__item__row" role="none">
                        <div class="munin-explorer-dataitem-main" role="none">
                        @RowChevron(v)
                        @RowHeading(v)
                        @RowSaveButton(v)
                        @InfoLine(v)
                        </div>@* munin-explorer-dataitem-main — the columns end here *@

                    @* No description in the row. Neither reference has one: Runa's rows are a
                       single table row, and helsedata's are explicitly one line —
                       `.munin-explorer-dataitem-main { height: 3.5rem; overflow: hidden; text-overflow:
                       ellipsis }`. A paragraph under the columns fights both. The description is
                       the first field in the panel, one click away. *@

                    </div>@* munin-explorer-data-list__item__row — the panel below is its SIBLING,
                              not its child, which is how helsedata nests it *@

                        @* The panel itself, drawn inside the row it belongs to — no routing, no
                           NavigationManager, nothing for the host's CMS to route. Only one row is
                           ever open, so this is at most one extra region landmark on the page, and
                           it is named after the variable rather than left anonymous among the host
                           page's own.

                           `munin-explorer-detail` is a handle, like `munin-explorer` and
                           `munin-explorer-filters`: nothing in this package or in Stiler defines
                           a rule for it. What dresses the panel is base styling for <dl class="munin-explorer-meta__grid munin-explorer-meta__grid-2">, <ol> and
                           <ul>, for the reason the filter panel is built from <details> and <ul> —
                           Stiler has no definition list, no breadcrumb and no key/value block whose
                           names can be read back off its compiled stylesheet, and a name it has
                           never heard of renders as a raw browser default. *@
                        @if (IsSelected(v))
                        {
                            @* A cell around the panel, wearing nothing else. The row above owns
                               everything under the list item, and a row owns only cells — the same
                               rule that puts an expanded panel in a <td colspan> in a real table.
                               It is this bare wrapper rather than a role on the panel itself so
                               the panel keeps being a region: a landmark named after the variable
                               is how a reader finds which one they opened, and a cell cannot be
                               both. The element carries no class on purpose; it lays nothing out. *@
                            <div role="cell">
                            @* aria-labelledby points at the button that opens this panel, which is
                               the element carrying the variable's name now that no heading wraps it.

                               Note where this comment sits. A Razor comment between an element's
                               ATTRIBUTES is emitted as an attribute name, and the browser throws
                               InvalidCharacterError from setAttribute - the panel then never opens.
                               bUnit renders it without complaint, so the suite stays green and only
                               a real browser shows the failure. Comments go above the element. *@
                            <div class="munin-explorer-meta munin-explorer-detail"
                                 id="@DetailId(v)"
                                 role="region"
                                 aria-labelledby="@DetailToggleId(v)"
                                 aria-busy="@DetailBusy">
                                @* One line, holding one message at a time: loading, or why the panel
                                   is empty. Its own region rather than the component's — the status
                                   line above describes the list, and a panel that failed to load has
                                   not changed which rows are on screen. Polite, not an alert: the
                                   reader asked for this one card and nothing else is affected. *@
                                <p class="@DetailStatusClass"
                                   role="status"
                                   aria-live="polite"
                                   aria-atomic="true">@DetailStatus</p>

                                @if (_detail is { } detail)
                                {
                                    @* Runa's two tabs, in helsedata's vocabulary. The split is
                                       Runa's — what the variable IS, and what its data holds —
                                       because Runa is what replaces helsedata's variable page, so
                                       it decides the content. helsedata decides the dress:
                                       munin-explorer-meta__tabs, __tab, __tab--active, __body and
                                       Stiler's hd-button-reset are Stiler's, and munin-explorer-meta
                                       carries 204 rules.

                                       Only the selected tab's button is in the tab order — the
                                       others carry tabindex="-1" — so the tablist costs one tab
                                       stop rather than one per tab. Arrow keys move between them,
                                       which is what the APG tabs pattern replaces those stops
                                       with; without it the panel would be unreachable by keyboard
                                       rather than merely awkward. *@
                                    <div class="munin-explorer-meta__tabs"
                                         role="tablist"
                                         aria-label="@T.TabDetails"
                                         @onkeydown="TabKey">
                                        @foreach (var tab in Tabs)
                                        {
                                            <div class="@TabClass(tab)" @key="tab">
                                                <button class="hd-button-reset"
                                                        type="button"
                                                        role="tab"
                                                        id="@TabId(tab)"
                                                        aria-selected="@(tab == _tab ? "true" : "false")"
                                                        aria-controls="@TabPanelId()"
                                                        tabindex="@(tab == _tab ? 0 : -1)"
                                                        @onclick="() => SelectTab(tab)">@TabLabel(tab)</button>
                                            </div>
                                        }
                                    </div>

                                    @* Unnamed on purpose. This wrapper carried a name in helsedata's own meta family
                                         that they never defined a rule for, so it promised styling that
                                         existed nowhere. Removed rather than renamed: after the
                                         munin-explorer rename it would be ours, and we would then owe it
                                         a rule it does not need.

                                         The name is deliberately not written here. The CSS check reads
                                         prose as well as markup, on purpose, so a name mentioned in a
                                         comment is a name it goes looking for a rule for. *@
                                      <div>
                                        <div class="munin-explorer-meta__tab-content"
                                             role="tabpanel"
                                             id="@TabPanelId()"
                                             aria-labelledby="@TabId(_tab)"
                                             tabindex="0">
                                            @if (_tab is PanelTab.Details)
                                            {
                                                @* Runa's three groups: Identifikasjon, Plassering
                                                   and Egenskaper, in that order. *@
                                                <h3 class="headline headline-xxs margin--none munin-explorer-group">@T.GroupIdentification</h3>

                                                <dl class="munin-explorer-meta__grid munin-explorer-meta__grid-1">
                                                    <div>
                                                        <dt class="headline headline-xxs margin--none">@T.FieldDescription</dt>
                                                        <dd>@DetailValue(detail.Description)</dd>
                                                    </div>
                                                </dl>

                                                <h3 class="headline headline-xxs margin--none munin-explorer-group">@T.GroupPlacement</h3>

                                                @* Side by side rather than stacked, which is how
                                                   Runa lays these three out — she uses three lanes,
                                                   helsedata's grid is two (`1fr 1fr`, collapsing to
                                                   one below 1280px). Two of theirs beats three of
                                                   ours: the arrangement is Runa's idea, the
                                                   proportions and the breakpoint are helsedata's,
                                                   and no new class name is needed for it.

                                                   Each field is wrapped in a <div>, which a grid
                                                   needs and the HTML spec allows inside <dl>.
                                                   Without the wrapper the grid would take dt and dd
                                                   as separate cells and lay out label | value
                                                   across, instead of label-over-value in a lane. *@
                                                <dl class="munin-explorer-meta__grid">
                                                    <div>
                                                        <dt class="headline headline-xxs margin--none">@T.FieldKildePath</dt>
                                                        <dd>@KildeTrail(detail)</dd>
                                                    </div>

                                                    <div>
                                                        <dt class="headline headline-xxs margin--none">@T.FieldVariableGroup</dt>
                                                        <dd>@NameList(VariabelgruppeNames(detail))</dd>
                                                    </div>

                                                    <div>
                                                        <dt class="headline headline-xxs margin--none">@T.FieldDataPeriod</dt>
                                                        <dd>@PeriodBar(detail.DataFrom, detail.DataTo)</dd>
                                                    </div>
                                                </dl>

                                                @* Runa's third group. Its contents are entirely
                                                   the catalogue's: which properties exist, what
                                                   they are called, what order they come in and
                                                   what their coded values mean all arrive with the
                                                   payload, so a property added in Munin shows up
                                                   here without this package being touched.

                                                   The values arrive as codes — "Opprinnelse": "5" —
                                                   and are resolved against the vocabulary that
                                                   travels beside them. Nothing is resolved here
                                                   against a table of our own; see PropertyValue. *@
                                                @PropertiesGroup(detail)
                                            }
                                            else
                                            {
                                                @* Runa's arrangement: the links grouped under a
                                                   heading per kind of kodeverk, each line carrying
                                                   its own reference and — where the API serves them
                                                   — the control that fetches the codes.

                                                   No <dl> around it any more. The kind was a label
                                                   and the kodeverk its value while the panel showed
                                                   one flat line each; grouped, the kind is a
                                                   heading over a list, and a definition list whose
                                                   value is another list of labelled things says
                                                   less than the headings do.

                                                   The codes themselves are not in this payload and
                                                   are not asked for until a reader presses for
                                                   them. Kommunenummer alone is 885 codes. *@
                                                @KodeverkGroups(detail)
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                    </div>

                                    @* The way on to the kilde and the datasamling the variable
                                       belongs to. One press further in, in the same place and by
                                       the same means as the panel around it — no routing, no
                                       NavigationManager, nothing for the host's CMS to route.

                                       Buttons under the trail rather than the trail's own steps
                                       made clickable. A step here is a <li> in a bare <ol> that a
                                       host draws as a path with chevrons between the steps, and
                                       the only button Stiler gives this package is the square
                                       button — which inside that flex row would break the path
                                       into a stack of filled blocks. The trail keeps saying where
                                       the variable sits; these two say what to press.

                                       Rendered only for an owner the variable actually names, so
                                       neither button can be one that could only ever report "not
                                       found". Never disabled, including while its own fetch runs,
                                       for the reason the disclosure above it is not: pressing it
                                       again is how the panel closes, and disabling the element
                                       that has focus drops focus to <body>. *@
                                    @foreach (var kind in SourceTargets(detail))
                                    {
                                        <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-right margin-bottom"
                                                type="button"
                                                id="@SourceToggleId(kind)"
                                                aria-expanded="@SourceExpanded(kind)"
                                                aria-controls="@SourceControls(kind)"
                                                @onclick="() => ToggleSourceAsync(kind)">@SourceToggleText(kind)</button>
                                    }

                                    @* After the owner buttons, not before. Those two are the
                                       narrower move — one part of this variable's surroundings —
                                       and going to the whole variable is the wider one, so it reads
                                       last. It also keeps the owner buttons where they have always
                                       been, which the tests that count them rely on. *@
                                    <button class="hd-button-square button-square--ghost margin-right margin-bottom"
                                            type="button"
                                            aria-expanded="false"
                                            aria-controls="@WholeVariableId"
                                            @onclick="ToggleWholeVariableAsync">@T.ShowWholeVariable</button>

                                    @* The owner's own record, drawn inside the variable's panel.
                                       One at a time — pressing Datasamling with Datakilde open
                                       swaps it — so this is at most one further region on the
                                       page, named after the thing it describes rather than left
                                       anonymous among the host page's own.

                                       The drill-in that opens is named by the id
                                       `munin-explorer-source-<instance>` and wears the class
                                       `munin-explorer-drilldown` — a handle like the others,
                                       and like them nothing in this package, in Stiler or in
                                       helsedata's variables.css defines a rule for it.
                                       What dresses the panel is base styling for <dl class="munin-explorer-meta__grid munin-explorer-meta__grid-2">, exactly as
                                       for the variable's own panel above. *@
                                }
                            </div>@* munin-explorer-meta munin-explorer-detail *@
                            </div>@* the row's last cell *@
                        }
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }
    </div>@* munin-explorer-data-list__result *@
    </div>@* munin-explorer-results *@
    </div>@* munin-explorer-container *@

    @* The pager. Forrige / Neste with the position between them, and no infinite scrolling —
       Munin's own explorer pages the same way, and a list that grows as you scroll has no end
       for a keyboard user to reach.

       Only when there is more than one page, or when a pager button is what put the reader here.
       "Side 1 av 1" between two buttons that can never do anything is furniture, and a single page
       reached by a new search or a new ordering costs nothing to leave out — neither is started
       from a pager button. A retreat is: an index that shrank to one page's worth between two
       requests answers Neste with an empty page and lands back on page 1 with TotalPages == 1, and
       dropping the pager in that render would take Neste out of the document under the finger that
       pressed it. ShowPager is where those two cases are told apart.

       Outside the rows' own guard on purpose. Tied to the rows it would vanish under the finger
       that pressed it the moment a page came back empty — an index that shrank between two
       requests, or an API that answers an out-of-range page with 404 — taking the focused button
       with it. RetreatFromEmptyPageAsync is what stops that state from lasting; this is what makes
       it survivable while it does.

       `munin-explorer-pagination` and `munin-explorer-pagination-content` are ours, like the rest
       of the prefix. They were helsedata's `variables-pagination*` until `Fhi.Metadata-hyyxl`,
       kept back when the rest of the vocabulary was renamed because Stiler had no pagination rule
       of any kind and helsedata's own `variables.css` — loaded on every page of their site,
       despite the name — did. That reasoning only ever covered hosts inside their estate: a host
       with Stiler alone drew the pager at browser defaults, which is the whole failure the rename
       exists to end. The rules ship in Stiler under `components/munin-explorer/` with the rest.
       The skip link above is the one name from that file left over, and still helsedata's.

       A div with role="navigation" rather than a <nav>: the landmark is worth having, and it
       is named so it does not sit anonymously among the host page's own. tabindex="-1" makes it
       the skip link's target — focusable programmatically, and still not a tab stop. *@
    @if (ShowPager)
    {
        <div class="munin-explorer-pagination"
             id="@PaginationId"
             tabindex="-1"
             role="navigation"
             aria-label="@T.Pagination">
            <div class="munin-explorer-pagination-content">
                @* aria-disabled, not disabled — see AriaDisabled in the codebehind. Both
                   buttons ask for a page that does not exist at the ends of the list; the
                   clamp in GoToPageAsync is what makes that inert, so the boundary is one
                   rule in one place rather than a condition repeated on each button. *@
                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--secondary"
                        type="button"
                        aria-label="@T.PreviousLabel"
                        aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(CanGoPrevious)"
                        @onclick="() => GoToPageAsync(_page - 1)">@T.Previous</button>

                @* Not a live region of its own. Turning a page rewrites the status line above,
                   which is already polite and atomic and already says which rows are on
                   screen, so a second announcement here would say the same thing twice. *@
                <span class="caption">@T.PageOf(_page, TotalPages)</span>

                <button class="hd-button-square button-square--secondary"
                        type="button"
                        aria-label="@T.NextLabel"
                        aria-disabled="@AriaDisabled(CanGoNext)"
                        @onclick="() => GoToPageAsync(_page + 1)">@T.Next</button>

                @* Buttons rather than a <select>: no name for one can be read back off Stiler, so
                   the shape changes instead of the package shipping CSS. aria-pressed rather than
                   a radiogroup, whose arrow-key navigation would need script we do not ship. *@
                <div class="munin-explorer-pagination-size"
                     role="group"
                     aria-labelledby="@PageSizeLabelId">
                    @* Named by the words on screen rather than by an aria-label, so the group has
                       one name and sighted readers can see it too. *@
                    <span class="caption" id="@PageSizeLabelId">@T.VariablesPerPage</span>
                    @foreach (var size in PageSizeOptions)
                    {
                        <button class="@PageSizeClass(size)"
                                type="button"
                                aria-label="@T.VariablesPerPageLabel(size)"
                                aria-pressed="@(ClampedPageSize == size ? "true" : "false")"
                                @onclick="() => SetPageSizeAsync(size)">@size</button>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    }
}
</section>
